# CMAT research AI handoff

This file defines repository-wide conventions for AI-assisted work, with special emphasis on paper authoring and reproducible figures. It should remain present on every maintained long-lived branch so that paper branches do not silently drift in style or build behaviour.

## Repository and branch discipline

- `main` owns reusable scientific code, shared plotting style, data contracts, and repository-wide conventions.
- `brainstorm/proyecto-visitas` is the shared exploratory workspace.
- `paper/paper1-ppa-persistence`, `paper/paper2-mu-performance`, `paper/paper3-grading-heterogeneity`, `paper/paper4-degree-help-seeking`, and `paper/paper5-longitudinal-trajectories` own paper-specific prose, analyses, and outputs.
- When a reusable method or plotting convention changes, implement/review it in `main` and then sync the long-lived branches. Do not let paper branches develop incompatible copies of shared scientific utilities.
- Preserve each paper's scientific scope. Formatting synchronization must not silently move estimands, analyses, or claims between papers.

## Canonical paper outputs

When a branch contains a LaTeX paper under `paper/`:

- `paper/manuscript.tex` is the single canonical manuscript source (it may itself include section files).
- `paper/main.tex` is the official clean wrapper and must hide development TODOs.
- `paper/main_commented.tex` is the development wrapper and must show TODOs.
- Do not maintain two independent prose copies. Both wrappers must compile the same `manuscript.tex` content.
- `paper/references.bib` is the paper bibliography database.
- `python paper/build.py` must build both `main.pdf` and `main_commented.pdf` whenever a buildable manuscript exists.
- Put development tasks in `\draftnote{...}`. Do not leave TODO prose as ordinary manuscript text when it is not intended for submission.

A standard wrapper pattern is:

```tex
% main.tex
\def\PaperCommented{0}
\input{manuscript.tex}
```

and:

```tex
% main_commented.tex
\def\PaperCommented{1}
\input{manuscript.tex}
```

The manuscript preamble should define `\draftnote` conditionally from `\PaperCommented`.

## Page, author, and typography conventions

- Use `\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}` unless a target journal template later overrides it.
- Use explicit A4 margins of 1.8 cm on all sides with `geometry` for the current house draft.
- Author name: `Heriberto Espino-Montelongo`.
- Affiliation: `Universidad de las Américas Puebla` unless a journal requires anonymization.
- Use Latin Modern/Computer Modern in LaTeX; do not commit or distribute font files.

## Citations and cross-references

- References are numeric in current drafts.
- Use `\usepackage[numbers,sort&compress]{natbib}` and `\bibliographystyle{unsrtnat}`. Do not depend on `IEEEtranN.bst` unless CI is deliberately changed to install and test it.
- Load `hyperref` before `cleveref`.
- Use `\usepackage[nameinlink,noabbrev]{cleveref}`.
- Prefer `\cref{...}` / `\Cref{...}` for figures, tables, equations, sections, and appendices instead of manual forms such as `Figure~\ref{...}`.
- Use semantic labels: `fig:...`, `tab:...`, `eq:...`, `sec:...`, and `app:...`.
- Ordinary literature citations should remain `\citep{...}` / `\citet{...}` as grammatically appropriate; `cleveref` is for document cross-references, not bibliography citations.

## Mathematics

- Every standalone/displayed mathematical expression must use a numbered, referenceable environment, normally `equation`; use `align` only when multiple aligned lines are genuinely needed.
- Every displayed equation must have a `\label{eq:...}`.
- Refer to displayed equations with `\cref`/`\Cref` whenever the prose discusses or reuses them.
- Do not use `\[...\]`, `$$...$$`, `equation*`, or other unnumbered display environments in paper manuscripts.
- Inline notation and routine inline statistics such as `$p<.001$`, `$R^2$`, `$Z$`, or `$N=6627$` remain inline.

## Tables and appendices

- Keep readable tables in the main text when they fit cleanly.
- Wide or highly detailed tables that would create material `overfull` boxes belong in an appendix rather than being shrunk to illegible sizes.
- Use `pdflscape` and a `landscape` environment for genuinely wide appendix tables so the PDF page has correct landscape orientation metadata.
- Main-text prose may forward-reference appendix tables with `\Cref{tab:...}`.
- After layout changes, inspect both the LaTeX log and rendered PDF rather than relying only on successful compilation.

## Figures and shared plotting style

The canonical plotting style is `cmat_analysis.visualization.style` and should be synchronized from `main`.

- Matplotlib/Seaborn: use Seaborn `whitegrid` with `context="paper"`.
- Serif font resolution: prefer `CMU Serif`, then `Latin Modern Roman`, then `DejaVu Serif` so CI remains reproducible when CMU is unavailable.
- Mathematical text in Matplotlib should use Computer Modern via `mathtext.fontset="cm"`.
- Use a light Plotly publication template aligned with the paper figures; do not retain an unrelated dark Plotly theme in the shared publication style.
- Do not commit font files merely to force a local font choice.
- Styling code must affect presentation only; it must never alter cohorts, variables, estimands, or statistical calculations.
- Figure recipes should call the shared style rather than reimplementing fonts/grid/theme ad hoc in each paper.

## Build and QA checklist

Before treating a paper build as clean:

- compile both official and commented versions;
- verify BibTeX resolves all citations;
- verify `cleveref` resolves all document labels;
- confirm the official PDF contains no visible TODO annotations;
- inspect the log for material `Overfull \\hbox` / `Overfull \\vbox` warnings;
- verify wide appendix tables are legible and correctly oriented;
- visually inspect the PDF after typography, margin, table, or figure changes;
- keep scientific interpretation constraints and observational/causal language unchanged unless the underlying design actually changes.

## Current paper-state note

Paper 1–4 have buildable LaTeX manuscripts and should follow the official/commented convention above. Paper 5 currently does not contain a buildable manuscript; do not invent placeholder scientific prose solely to satisfy the format. When its manuscript is created, start directly from these conventions.
